# Line endings, fixed in the repository rather than left to each contributor's
# `core.autocrlf`.
#
# The 2026-08 review recommended this and priced it at "a one-time whole-tree
# normalisation commit". Measured before writing the file: of 268 tracked files,
# the only ones holding a CR in the index are the PDFs, fonts and icons below —
# **no text file is stored with CRLF**. So the normalisation cost is zero, and
# this file is purely a guard that keeps it that way when someone commits from a
# Windows checkout with `core.autocrlf=true`.
#
# The churn the review actually saw came from `app/.flutter-plugins-dependencies`,
# a generated file that records absolute pub-cache paths and was tracked despite
# `app/.gitignore` naming it. It is untracked now, which removes the source
# rather than normalising the symptom.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells are the exception. cmd.exe mis-parses an LF-only batch file in
# ways that surface as a label or `goto` silently doing nothing — and
# `sync-core.bat` is a build script, so a subtle failure there costs someone an
# afternoon.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.xz binary
*.gz binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary

# The formats we read. Committed fixtures must survive byte-for-byte, or an
# importer test is asserting against a file git quietly rewrote.
*.one binary
*.onepkg binary
*.onote binary
*.cab binary
